      "1": "# 3Sum — Sorting + Two Pointers\n\n## 1. Problem Understanding\nGiven an integer array `nums`, return all **unique triplets** `[a, b, c]` such that:\n- `a + b + c == 0`\n- Each triplet uses three elements at different indices.\n- The result must not contain duplicate triplets.\n\nExample:\n```python\nnums = [-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]\n# Output: [[-1, -1, 2], [-1, 0, 1]]\n```\nBoth triplets sum to zero. Although `-1` appears twice in the input, the triplet `[-1, 0, 1]` should be returned only once.\n**Key constraint:** With `n` up to 3000, an `O(n^2)` solution is appropriate, but an `O(n^3)` brute-force solution is too slow.\n\n## 2. Why the Naive Approach Fails\nThe brute-force approach uses three nested loops to examine every possible triplet:\n```python\nfor i in range(n):\n    for j in range(i + 1, n):\n        for k in range(j + 1, n):\n            if nums[i] + nums[j] + nums[k] == 0:\n                # Store the triplet\n```\nThere are approximately \\(n^3/6\\) possible triplets, giving **`O(n^3)` time complexity**. For `n = 3000`, that is roughly 4.5 billion combinations.\nA set could remove duplicate results, but it would not solve the performance problem caused by the three nested loops.\n**Goal:** Reduce the time complexity to `O(n^2)` while avoiding duplicate triplets during the search.\n\n## 3. Think of the Problem as 2Sum Repeatedly\nThe key insight is that **3Sum can be converted into a 2Sum problem**.\nFor every fixed value `nums[i]`, we need to find two other values satisfying:\n\\[\nnums[left] + nums[right] = -nums[i]\n\\]\nInstead of checking every possible pair, sort the array and use two pointers:\n- `left` starts immediately after `i`.\n- `right` starts at the last index.\n- Move the pointers according to whether the current sum is too small, too large, or exactly zero.\nSorting also places duplicate values next to each other, making it easier to skip repeated triplets.\n\n## 4. Step-by-Step Approach\n1. **Sort the array:** Values are now arranged in ascending order.\n2. **Fix the first element:** Iterate through the array using `i`.\n3. **Stop when `nums[i] > 0`:** Since all remaining values are also positive, three values cannot sum to zero.\n4. **Skip duplicate first values:** If `nums[i] == nums[i - 1]`, skip this iteration because the previous occurrence has already been processed.\n5. **Initialize two pointers:** Set `left = i + 1` and `right = n - 1`.\n6. **Calculate the triplet sum:** Compute `total = nums[i] + nums[left] + nums[right]`.\n7. **Adjust the pointers:**\n   - If `total < 0`, move `left` rightward to increase the sum.\n   - If `total > 0`, move `right` leftward to decrease the sum.\n   - If `total == 0`, save the triplet and move both pointers inward.\n8. **Skip duplicate pair values:** After recording a triplet, skip repeated values at both pointers.\n9. Return `result`.\n\n## 5. Why Sorting and Two Pointers Are the Key\nConsider the sorted array:\n```python\nnums = [-4, -1, -1, 0, 1, 2]\n```\nSuppose `nums[i] = -1`, `nums[left] = -1`, and `nums[right] = 2`.\n\nThe sum is:\n\\[\n-1 + (-1) + 2 = 0\n\\]\nWe found `[-1, -1, 2]`.\nNow move both pointers inward. If the next left value is still `-1`, skip it because it would recreate the same triplet with the same fixed value and right value.\n\nThe pointer movement is based on a crucial property of sorted arrays:\n- Moving `left` to a larger value can only increase the sum.\n- Moving `right` to a smaller value can only decrease the sum.\n\nTherefore, we can eliminate impossible pairs without checking them individually.\n\n**Why the search does not miss valid pairs:**\n- If the sum is too small, keeping `left` and moving `right` inward would only make the sum smaller or equal. So `left` must advance.\n- If the sum is too large, keeping `right` and moving `left` inward would only make the sum larger or equal. So `right` must retreat.\n- When the sum is zero, we record the triplet and move both pointers to search for other pairs.\n\n## 6. How the Variables Work\n| Variable | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `nums` | The sorted input array. |\n| `n` | Number of elements in the array. |\n| `result` | Stores all unique triplets whose sum is zero. |\n| `i` | Index of the fixed first element. |\n| `left` | Pointer to the first candidate for the remaining pair. |\n| `right` | Pointer to the second candidate for the remaining pair. |\n| `total` | Sum of the three currently selected values. |\n\nThe three important conditions are:\n```python\nif total < 0:\n    left += 1\nelif total > 0:\n    right -= 1\nelse:\n    result.append([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]])\n```\nThe duplicate-skipping conditions serve a different purpose: they ensure that a valid triplet is not added more than once.\n\n## 7. How the Algorithm Works — Example Trace\nInput:\n```python\nnums = [-1, 0, 1, 2, -1, -4]\n```\nAfter sorting:\n```python\nnums = [-4, -1, -1, 0, 1, 2]\n```\n\n**First iteration: `i = 0`, fixed value `-4`**\n\n| Fixed | Left | Right | Sum | Action |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| -4 | -1 | 2 | -3 | Too small; move `left` |\n| -4 | -1 | 2 | -3 | The next `-1` is at the next index; move `left` again |\n| -4 | 0 | 2 | -2 | Too small; move `left` |\n| -4 | 1 | 2 | -1 | Too small; move `left`; pointers meet |\n\nNo valid triplet is found for `-4`.\n\n**Second iteration: `i = 1`, fixed value `-1`**\n\n| Fixed | Left | Right | Sum | Action |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| -1 | -1 | 2 | 0 | Record `[-1, -1, 2]`; move both pointers |\n| -1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | Record `[-1, 0, 1]`; move both pointers; stop |\n\nNow `i = 2` also has value `-1`. It is skipped because the same fixed value was already processed at index `1`.\n\nThe remaining fixed values are `0`, `1`, and `2`. Once the fixed value becomes positive, the algorithm stops.\n\nFinal result:\n```python\n[[-1, -1, 2], [-1, 0, 1]]\n```\n\n## 8. Time and Space Complexity\n**Time complexity: `O(n^2)`**\n- Sorting takes `O(n log n)`.\n- The outer loop runs at most `n` times.\n- For each fixed element, the two pointers move only inward, taking `O(n)` time.\n- Total: `O(n log n) + O(n^2) = O(n^2)`.\n\n**Auxiliary space complexity:** Depends on the sorting implementation.\n- The two-pointer search itself uses"
